     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-17 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Astronomers Identify Potential Isolated Dark Galaxy Candidate</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Using China's FAST radio telescope, astronomers have discovered J1351+0039, an isolated cloud of neutral hydrogen containing nearly 500,000 solar masses. Located far from large galaxies, the object shows no signs of stars or tidal origins, making it a strong candidate for a &quot;dark galaxy&quot; or reionization-limited H I cloud.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-17/astronomers-identify-potential-isolated-dark-galaxy-candidat.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Deep optical observations from the DESI Legacy Survey confirmed the absence of stellar counterparts, suggesting the system is dominated by dark matter. With a temperature of 10,000 Kelvin, the cloud remains in equilibrium with the ultraviolet background, consistent with theoretical models of starless halos that never cooled sufficiently to ignite star formation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the core scientific findings and context regarding the J1351+0039 discovery.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-lone-starless-cloud-dark-galaxy.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
