     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-17 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Launches Unified Form 141 for TDS Reporting</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#business</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Indian Income Tax Department has introduced Form 141, a unified reporting document that replaces four previously separate TDS forms: 26QB, 26QC, 26QD, and 26QE. This consolidation aims to simplify compliance for taxpayers handling transactions involving immovable property, rent payments, professional fees, and virtual digital assets. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-17/india-launches-unified-form-141-for-tds-reporting.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Effective October 1, 2026, the new form covers five distinct schedules based on transaction type. Taxpayers must note that Form 141 allows for only one transaction type per filing, requiring separate forms for different categories. The update streamlines reporting for both resident and non-resident deductees, providing a more user-friendly experience for those responsible for deducting and depositing tax at source.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief captures the essential details of Form 141 and its purpose accurately based on the provided text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/wealth/tax/transferring-immovable-property-how-tds-compliance-is-now-simpler-with-new-form-141/articleshow/135008328.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
