     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-17 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Indravati Tiger Reserve Reopens for Safaris After 40 Years</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Chhattisgarh</span> <span>#Tiger Reserve</span> <span>#Indravati</span> <span>#Wildlife</span></div>
       <p>Indravati Tiger Reserve in Chhattisgarh is preparing to reopen for organised safaris on November 1, 2026, after remaining inaccessible to wildlife lovers for nearly 40 years. The closure, despite abundant wildlife, was due to the dangers posed by Maoist insurgency and armed conflict in Bastar's Bijapur district, which made travel risky even for forest officials after its declaration as a tiger reserve in 1983. Improved security conditions are now paving the way for its revival as a tourism destination.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-17/indravati-tiger-reserve-reopens-for-safaris-after-40-years.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Spanning 2,799 sq km, Indravati is one of Central India's largest tiger reserves, sheltering diverse wildlife including tigers, leopards, sloth bears, gaurs, wild dogs, and deer. It also serves as a crucial habitat for the endangered wild water buffalo, Chhattisgarh's state animal. Current figures from the forest department indicate six tigers, with a fresh population assessment underway. The reserve's long absence from tourism has also meant that portions of its wildlife remain poorly documented.</p><p>Recent research in 2025 led to a remarkable discovery: researchers found smooth-coated otters, locally known as 'neer billi', in the Indravati River. This provided the first photographic evidence of these Vulnerable otters in roughly 25 years, with 10 individuals spotted feeding on fish one February morning. While their presence suggests a healthy river ecosystem, evidence of hunting and snares highlights the urgent need for protection. The forest department is planning a cautious reopening, starting with two or three safari destinations based on the reserve's Tiger Conservation Plan, and will implement restrictions on tourist numbers and vehicles after comprehensive wildlife and security assessments.</p><p>Beyond conservation, the reopening offers new opportunities for local communities around Indravati. Officials are exploring ways to involve residents as guides, drivers, and hospitality providers, alongside potential for homestays and the sale of local products. This integration aims to ensure that local families benefit economically, fostering a sustainable model where both the wildlife and human populations thrive long after the initial safaris begin.</p><p>Why it matters: This reopening balances conservation with community development after decades of inaccessibility.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is excellent, capturing all essential figures, dates, and context while maintaining a well-organized narrative flow.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/travel/indravati-tiger-reserve-chhattisgarh-reopen-dates-tourists-safaris-after-40-years-12680513" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
