     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-10-17 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>InvoiceCloud Invests ₹350 Crore to Expand Hyderabad Innovation Hub</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#InvoiceCloud</span> <span>#Hyderabad</span> <span>#Investment</span> <span>#AI</span></div>
       <p>Digital billing and payment solutions provider InvoiceCloud has announced a three-year growth plan, committing approximately ₹350 crore to strengthen its global innovation hub in Hyderabad. This investment aims to support team expansion, infrastructure development, artificial intelligence (AI) initiatives, and product development, building on the India team's contributions to customer solutions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-17/invoicecloud-invests-350-crore-to-expand-hyderabad-innovatio.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>InvoiceCloud's CEO, Kevin O'Brien, stated that Hyderabad is becoming a larger part of the company's customer-building strategy, driven by the team's results. He added that the investment provides &quot;the people, technology and capacity to build on that progress over the next three years.&quot; Since establishing operations in Hyderabad in 2024, the India team has grown to over 250 employees, with projections to reach 300 by the end of 2026 and further growth planned. More than 70 per cent of Indian employees are in engineering and product roles, supporting AI, platform engineering, and product development.</p><p>The Hyderabad teams are advancing InvoiceCloud’s AI roadmap across product development, customer experience, platform reliability, and technology modernization. Per-engineer productivity has increased 120 per cent year-on-year, attributed largely to AI tools and processes that facilitate faster solution deployment. The Hyderabad team also developed InvoiceCloud’s agentic AI customer self-service capability, launching it in under six months. Biju Davis, Senior Vice-President and India Site Leader, highlighted this as an example of combining engineering talent with AI.</p><p>InvoiceCloud has also expanded its Hyderabad footprint by 50 per cent to 33,000 square feet, providing additional space for its engineering, product, and AI teams. The new floor features a permanent mural co-created by employees and the Aravani Art Project. The inauguration saw over 20 leaders from the US headquarters and India team, including CEO Kevin O'Brien and India Site Leader Biju Davis, attend. Headquartered in the US, InvoiceCloud serves more than 3,250 customers across utility, government, and insurance sectors, processing over $40 billion in payments annually.</p><p>What to watch: Continued growth and AI advancements from InvoiceCloud's Hyderabad hub.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the growth plan, investment amount, and specific operational achievements from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/companies/start-ups/invoicecloud-hyderabad-investment-ai-product-development-india-126101700595_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
