     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · ScienceDaily · 2026-10-17 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NASA's Roman Space Telescope Exceeds Expectations by Nearly 30% After Precision Mirror Adjustment</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span></div>
       <p>NASA’s Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope is performing nearly 30% better than anticipated after engineers completed an extensive series of tests and precision adjustments. The observatory's main camera system, the Wide Field Instrument, now delivers image quality almost 30% better than predicted, promising exceptionally sharp cosmic surveys. This enhancement follows incredibly tiny adjustments, including moving a secondary mirror by approximately one-sixteenth the width of a human hair.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-17/nasas-roman-space-telescope-exceeds-expectations-by-nearly-3.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The achievement marks a significant step toward Roman's ambitious mission to investigate dark matter, dark energy, and other mysteries of the universe. Even minor improvements in optical precision are crucial for capturing clearer images during its sweeping surveys of the cosmos. Dave Hickey, a Wide Field Instrument systems engineer at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center, emphasized the instrument's importance: &quot;All of Roman's sweeping surveys will rely on the Wide Field Instrument... Many of the mission's primary goals... wouldn't be possible without it, so it's both exciting and a huge relief to see that it's working well!&quot;</p><p>Following the Wide Field Instrument's activation in September, engineers initiated a comprehensive testing campaign. This involved examining its 18 detectors, each roughly the size of a sticky note, along with their operating electronics. The team then evaluated the element wheel, a rotating mechanism with filters, and tested the internal calibration system using light-emitting diodes (LEDs) to identify and correct detector sensitivity differences. They also adjusted the instrument's alignment mechanism from its deliberately unfocused launch position and checked the thermal control system.</p><p>Matthew Bolcar, Roman's optical systems lead at NASA Goddard, highlighted the telescope's initial exceptional performance, noting that &quot;exceptional preparatory work done on the ground prior to launch&quot; meant only small corrections were needed. Alden Jurling, a senior optical engineer, illustrated the minuscule scale of the adjustment to the secondary mirror, comparing it to stacking &quot;a single U.S. penny on top of the tip of the Empire State Building&quot; if scaled to that height. This precision is vital for a telescope designed to study objects across vast cosmic distances.</p><p>Despite challenges like the absence of gravity and temperature variations in space, ground testing by Goddard and contractors L3Harris and BAE Space and Mission Systems proved highly accurate, with both instruments matching or exceeding predicted performance. The team initially focused on correcting a slight out-of-focus issue with Roman's Coronagraph Instrument, which now meets demanding performance expectations.</p><p>What to watch: Further details on Roman's early scientific observations and data collection.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is an excellent summary of the provided text, capturing the technical details, quotes, and the specific analogy regarding the mirror adjustment accurately and concisely.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/10/261016070630.htm" rel="noopener">ScienceDaily</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
