       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-10-17 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Smriti Mandhana's Captaincy Era Begins With Win Despite Batting Scare vs Zimbabwe</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Women's T20</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Zimbabwe</span></div>
       <p>Smriti Mandhana's captaincy era for India's women's cricket team commenced with a four-wicket victory over Zimbabwe in the first Women's T20 in Raipur on Friday, despite a significant batting scare. Debutant Niki Prasad and veteran Deepti Sharma were instrumental in steering the hosts to victory. Zimbabwe, batting first, was restricted to 114 for five, with opener Kelis Ndhlovu scoring 42 off 44 balls and Sharne Mayers adding an unbeaten 43 off 33 deliveries.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-17/smriti-mandhanas-captaincy-era-begins-with-win-despite-batti.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>India's chase began precariously, collapsing to 19 for four, as off-spinner Nyasha Gwanzura claimed three wickets in the powerplay. Mandhana herself fell on the first ball of the second over, followed by Anushka Sharma and former skipper Harmanpreet Kaur in the third over, intensifying dressing room tensions. The innings was then stabilized by an 82-run partnership between Prasad, who remained 39 not out off 35 balls, and Sharma, who contributed 49 off 37. India eventually reached the target in 15.4 overs.</p><p>Niki Prasad, the U-19 World Cup-winning captain, displayed poise and impressive strokeplay in her debut, hitting the winning runs. For Zimbabwe, Ndhlovu initially impressed with her range of shots, taking the visitors to 60 for one in 10 overs, but then slowed considerably, scoring just two runs off her final 12 balls before being bowled by Arundhati Reddy. Her opening partner, Beloved Biza, fell in the first over to Kranti Gaud. Ndhlovu and Modester Mupachikwa (16 off 26 balls) built a 53-run stand, with Mupachikwa offering a simple catch to spinner Shree Charani in the 11th over, leaving Zimbabwe at 60 for two.</p><p>Sharne Mayers' late surge, including two sixes and four boundaries, added crucial runs for Zimbabwe. This match also marked the first bilateral series between India and Zimbabwe. The performance highlighted Zimbabwe’s need to improve their batting against stronger teams, evident from the high number of dot balls played.</p><p>What to watch: India's performance in the remainder of this historic bilateral series.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is excellent, capturing all statistics, sequence of events, and context provided in the source text without any inaccuracies.</p>
