    </div>
  </div>
  <nav class="catrow"><span class="catgrp"><b>News</b><a href="/category/national">national</a><a href="/category/world">world</a><a href="/category/defence">defence</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Money</b><a href="/category/business">business</a><a href="/category/finance">finance</a><a href="/category/education">education</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Sports</b><a href="/category/sports">sports</a><a href="/category/cricket">cricket</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Culture</b><a href="/category/entertainment">entertainment</a><a href="/category/positive">positive</a><a href="/category/funny">funny</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Life</b><a href="/category/science">science</a><a href="/category/environment">environment</a><a href="/category/space">space</a><a href="/category/health">health</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Tech</b><a href="/category/tech">tech</a><a href="/category/ai">ai</a></span></nav></div><div class="gtr"><span>Read in your language:</span><div id="google_translate_element"></div></div><div class="wrap"><main id="content"><p class="crumb"><a href="/">← Front page</a> · <a href="/archive">Archive</a> · <a href="/category/business">business</a></p>
     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-10-17 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Starlink Claims India Infrastructure Approval Amid Satcom Dispute</h1>
       
       <p>Starlink asserted its readiness to provide high-speed internet services in India on Friday, stating it has worked with Indian regulators, industry, and operators for over five years. The Elon Musk-led company declared it has built dedicated infrastructure in the country, including a network of dedicated systems, gateways, and other facilities, &quot;all with the requisite government approvals&quot; to exclusively serve Indian traffic safely and securely. Starlink's post on X also tagged a statement from Reliance Industries.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-17/starlink-claims-india-infrastructure-approval-amid-satcom-di.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Reliance Industries, led by Mukesh Ambani, earlier on Friday welcomed competition in satellite communications, including from Starlink, but emphasized that all players must comply with Indian laws. In its first direct response to Musk's recent criticisms, Reliance stated it was placing &quot;the facts on record,&quot; asserting it has never feared competition and sees it as the &quot;truest test of who serves the customer best.&quot; The company cited Jio's record, which entered India's telecom market in 2016 and gained over 530 million customers within ten years, offering digital infrastructure and affordable data.</p><p>The exchange unfolded amid a public dispute over competition in India's satellite communications market, following Musk's critiques of the landscape and questions about the competitive environment. Government sources have consistently maintained that any company operating in India must comply with the law of the land. Bharti Airtel Chairman Sunil Mittal also weighed in at the Bloomberg New Economy Forum 2026, stating his satellite communications venture, Eutelsat OneWeb, was &quot;significantly ahead&quot; of Starlink in the queue to launch services, having applied five years prior with its ground network ready for three years and satellites hovering for five.</p><p>Musk had previously accused powerful business interests of blocking Starlink's entry, even sarcastically addressing Mukesh Ambani as &quot;Prime Minister Ambani&quot; on X and questioning his influence. The Ministry of Communications rejected these charges as &quot;baseless and misconceived,&quot; clarifying that satellite operators must meet uniform regulatory requirements, including security conditions and spectrum allocation rules, before commercial services can begin. Communications Minister Jyotiraditya Scindia confirmed that Starlink, Reliance's Jio Satcom, and Bharti-backed Eutelsat OneWeb have secured satellite communications licences, but stressed that spectrum would be assigned only after a revenue-based charging framework is finalized and individual operators fulfill security requirements. The distinction between a licence and operational approval is central, as securing a licence does not automatically grant permission for commercial operations.</p><p>What to watch: Finalization of spectrum pricing and security clearances for satellite operators.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a thorough overview of the dispute between Starlink and local Indian competitors, accurately reflecting the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/industry/news/satcom-row-starlink-says-india-infra-has-govt-approvals-ready-for-launch-126101700036_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
